{
    "$schema_note": "Which environment variables referenced by config/*.php are NOT part of the deployed target runtime contract, and why. KEY NAMES AND REASONS ONLY \u2014 never a value, never a credential. The deployment target templates under infrastructure/templates/environment/ are the authoritative list of what IS in the contract; this file accounts for everything else, so the inventory is closed: a new env() in config/*.php must land in a template or here, and an entry here that config/*.php no longer reads must be removed.",
    "categories": {
        "laravel-framework-default": "Generic Laravel configuration the framework ships a working default for. Our deployed topology does not override it, and pinning it per target would mean maintaining a copy of a framework default with nothing to say about it.",
        "dormant-driver": "A driver, queue backend, cache store, log handler or mail transport Laravel supports and this deployment does not use. Promoting these into the target contract would tell an operator to supply credentials for infrastructure that does not exist \u2014 see the note below on why that is the wrong direction.",
        "vendor-tuning-default": "A fine-grained Sentry or Nightwatch option. The target templates declare the handful that differ per environment class \u2014 DSN, environment, sample rates, the enable switches \u2014 and leave the rest at the vendor's default deliberately, so a vendor upgrade that adds options does not become 80 new required keys.",
        "application-default": "One of RateGuru's own settings whose default lives in config/ and is correct for every target today. It is overridable per host but not required, so it is not part of the contract an operator must satisfy. A setting that ever needs to differ per target belongs in the templates instead \u2014 that is a deliberate move, and this category is where it moves FROM.",
        "local-or-build-only": "Meaningful only for local development or a build step, never on a deployed target."
    },
